-- ops: 10000000
--
-- R5 of the M5 row (`plans/static-dispatch-spike.md` §7): an ordering used
-- three generic levels below where it was chosen.
--
-- This is the **C1** form of `c0/R5EvidenceForwarding.beni`, and the pair
-- measures **lambda-passing versus evidence**. The C0 program builds a
-- LAMBDA in `tallyBy` that closes over a local `key`, which is itself a
-- lambda over the parameter `scale`, and threads it through
-- `level1 -> level2 -> tally` as an ordinary argument; `cmp x mark` inside
-- `tally` is then an indirect call through a parameter, and every comparison
-- pays two `key` calls on top of it.
--
-- Here all three levels carry `where a.compare : a, a -> Order` instead. The
-- comparator parameter is gone from every signature, one hidden evidence
-- parameter takes its place (§8.1), `x.compare mark` is the call site, and
-- **no closure is allocated and no `key` is called anywhere in the program**.
-- `tallyBy` keeps its name and its place — it is still where the ordering is
-- chosen — but choosing it is now naming the element type, so its `scale`
-- parameter has no work left to do and is gone with the closure it existed
-- to capture. That difference IS the row; the two programs are not meant to
-- be the same JavaScript.
--
-- **The checksum is the guard.** `scale` is 1 at the one call site in C0, so
-- `key` is the identity there and both programs count the same elements of
-- the same list against the same pivot with the same total order on `Int`.
-- `5000000` — 20 000 repetitions x 250 elements below the pivot — is what C0
-- prints, and if this program prints anything else the row is void and the
-- two are not comparable.
--
-- The call site is MONOMORPHIC here — one element type, one ordering — which
-- is the case V8 inlines best. R6 is the same shape gone megamorphic.
--
-- One op is one `compare` call: 500 elements x 20 000 repetitions, the
-- repetitions run as 40 x 500 because `List.range 1 20000` recursed past the
-- JavaScript stack when this was written. **The tail-call loop landed on
-- 2026-09-18**, so the split is now vestigial for a compiler at
-- or after it and still mandatory for the C0 baseline binary; it is left
-- alone because the `-- ops:` count and the checksum are cited.
import List
import Node exposing (Program)
import String


outerReps : Int
outerReps = 40


innerReps : Int
innerReps = 500


pivot : Int
pivot = 250


values : List Int
values = List.map (List.range 1 500) λn -> Int.mod (n * 7919) 499


-- Level 3: the work. Everything it knows about `a` arrives as evidence.
tally : List a, a -> Int
    where a.compare : a, a -> Order
tally xs mark = List.foldl xs 0 λx acc ->
    case x.compare mark of
        LT ->
            acc + 1

        _ ->
            acc


-- Level 2 and level 1: pure forwarding, which is the point. Each one is
-- generic in `a` and so cannot know what `compare` is; each one forwards the
-- obligation rather than a value it can see.
level2 : List a, a -> Int
    where a.compare : a, a -> Order
level2 xs mark = tally xs mark


level1 : List a, a -> Int
    where a.compare : a, a -> Order
level1 xs mark = level2 xs mark


-- Where the ordering is chosen. In C0 this builds two closures; here it
-- discharges one obligation with `Int`'s own `compare` and calls.
tallyBy : List Int, Int -> Int
tallyBy xs mark = level1 xs mark


main : Program
main =
    total = List.foldl (List.range 1 outerReps) 0 λ_ acc ->
        List.foldl (List.range 1 innerReps) acc λ_ n -> n + tallyBy values pivot
    Node.print (String.fromInt total)
